\par
# 1. INTRODUCTION\par
\par
Linux is an open-source, Unix-like operating-system platform that has become an important part of modern information technology. It was originally developed by **Linus Torvalds in 1991** as a kernel and has since become the foundation of numerous operating-system distributions.\par
\par
Linux differs from many desktop-oriented operating systems because of its strong emphasis on command-line administration, modularity, networking, automation, and user control.\par
\par
Linux can operate with a graphical user interface, but many Linux servers operate without a graphical desktop. Administrators can therefore manage these systems remotely through terminals and command-line tools.\par
\par
Linux is available in many distributions, including Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, Arch Linux, openSUSE, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, and others. Each distribution may have different default configurations and software-management tools, but the fundamental Linux concepts remain largely similar.\par
\par
The objective of this course was to develop both conceptual understanding and practical familiarity with Linux systems.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 2. OBJECTIVES OF THE COURSE\par
\par
The major objectives of the course were:\par
\par
* To understand Linux operating-system concepts.\par
* To understand Linux architecture.\par
* To learn the Linux file-system structure.\par
* To understand Linux distributions.\par
* To learn command-line operations.\par
* To manage files and directories.\par
* To understand users and groups.\par
* To understand Linux permissions.\par
* To perform basic administration tasks.\par
* To monitor processes and system resources.\par
* To understand package management.\par
* To understand Linux networking.\par
* To learn Shell/Bash fundamentals.\par
* To develop basic Bash scripts.\par
* To understand command pipelines and redirection.\par
* To perform practical Linux activities.\par
* To understand fundamental Linux security concepts.\par
* To develop a foundation for further study in system administration, cloud computing, networking, software development, and cybersecurity.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 3. LINUX FUNDAMENTALS\par
\par
## 3.1 What is Linux?\par
\par
Linux is an open-source Unix-like operating-system kernel. In common usage, the term Linux often refers to a complete operating system built around the Linux kernel.\par
\par
A Linux distribution generally contains:\par
\par
* Linux kernel\par
* System libraries\par
* System utilities\par
* Shell\par
* Package manager\par
* Applications\par
* Configuration tools\par
* Optional graphical environment\par
\par
The kernel is responsible for managing hardware and providing fundamental services to applications.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 3.2 Characteristics of Linux\par
\par
### Open Source\par
\par
Linux is developed using open-source software and licensing models. This allows users and developers to inspect and modify relevant source code according to the applicable licenses.\par
\par
### Multiuser\par
\par
Linux allows multiple user accounts to exist on the same system.\par
\par
### Multitasking\par
\par
Linux can run multiple processes concurrently.\par
\par
### Portable\par
\par
Linux has been adapted to numerous hardware architectures and computing environments.\par
\par
### Secure Permission System\par
\par
Linux provides a permission and ownership model that controls access to files and directories.\par
\par
### Command-Line Based Administration\par
\par
A large number of system-management operations can be performed through the terminal.\par
\par
### Networking Support\par
\par
Linux includes extensive networking functionality and tools.\par
\par
### Automation\par
\par
Shell scripting allows repetitive administrative tasks to be automated.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 4. LINUX OPERATING SYSTEM\par
\par
An operating system manages communication between computer hardware and software.\par
\par
Linux performs several important functions:\par
\par
* Process management\par
* Memory management\par
* File-system management\par
* Device management\par
* Network management\par
* User management\par
* Security enforcement\par
* Hardware communication\par
\par
A simplified structure is:\par
\par
```text\par
+----------------------------------+\par
|       User Applications          |\par
+----------------------------------+\par
|       Shell / GUI Environment    |\par
+----------------------------------+\par
|        System Libraries          |\par
+----------------------------------+\par
|          Linux Kernel            |\par
+----------------------------------+\par
|            Hardware              |\par
+----------------------------------+\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 5. LINUX ARCHITECTURE\par
\par
## 5.1 Hardware\par
\par
The hardware layer contains physical components such as:\par
\par
* CPU\par
* RAM\par
* Hard disk/SSD\par
* Network interfaces\par
* USB devices\par
* Display devices\par
* Input devices\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 5.2 Linux Kernel\par
\par
The kernel is the central component of the operating system.\par
\par
It manages:\par
\par
* CPU scheduling\par
* Memory\par
* Processes\par
* Hardware devices\par
* Networking\par
* File systems\par
* Device drivers\par
* System calls\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 5.3 System Libraries\par
\par
System libraries provide functions that applications can use to communicate with operating-system services.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 5.4 Shell\par
\par
The shell provides an interface through which users can execute commands.\par
\par
Examples include:\par
\par
* Bash\par
* Zsh\par
* Fish\par
* Dash\par
* Ksh\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 5.5 Applications\par
\par
Applications operate above the operating-system infrastructure and may include:\par
\par
* Web browsers\par
* Editors\par
* Development tools\par
* Database servers\par
* Web servers\par
* Monitoring tools\par
* Office applications\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 6. LINUX DISTRIBUTIONS\par
\par
A Linux distribution combines the Linux kernel with software packages and system-management tools.\par
\par
Common distributions include:\par
\par
### Ubuntu\par
\par
A widely used Debian-based distribution suitable for desktop, server, development, and educational environments.\par
\par
### Debian\par
\par
A long-established distribution known for its extensive package repository and strong emphasis on stability.\par
\par
### Fedora\par
\par
A community distribution sponsored by Red Hat and often used for development and experimentation with newer technologies.\par
\par
### Arch Linux\par
\par
A distribution emphasizing user control and a minimal base installation.\par
\par
### Rocky Linux and AlmaLinux\par
\par
Enterprise-oriented distributions designed to provide compatibility with the broader Red Hat ecosystem.\par
\par
The choice of distribution depends on the intended use, support requirements, administrative preferences, and technical environment.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 7. LINUX FILE SYSTEM\par
\par
Linux uses a hierarchical file system beginning at the root directory:\par
\par
```text\par
/\par
```\par
\par
Important directories include:\par
\par
| Directory | Description                            |\par
| --------- | -------------------------------------- |\par
| `/`       | Root of the file system                |\par
| `/home`   | Home directories for regular users     |\par
| `/root`   | Home directory of the root user        |\par
| `/etc`    | System configuration                   |\par
| `/var`    | Variable data, including many logs     |\par
| `/tmp`    | Temporary files                        |\par
| `/usr`    | User applications and system resources |\par
| `/dev`    | Device interfaces                      |\par
| `/proc`   | Process and kernel information         |\par
| `/sys`    | Kernel and device information          |\par
| `/boot`   | Boot-related files                     |\par
\par
Understanding this structure is essential for Linux administration.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 8. LINUX ADMINISTRATION\par
\par
Linux administration involves maintaining and managing a Linux computer or server.\par
\par
Typical administrative responsibilities include:\par
\par
* Creating users\par
* Managing groups\par
* Setting permissions\par
* Installing software\par
* Managing services\par
* Monitoring processes\par
* Managing storage\par
* Configuring networks\par
* Examining logs\par
* Maintaining system updates\par
* Troubleshooting system problems\par
* Implementing security controls\par
\par
Linux administrators frequently use command-line tools because they provide precise and efficient control over the system.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 9. USER AND GROUP MANAGEMENT\par
\par
Linux is a multiuser operating system.\par
\par
## 9.1 Identifying the Current User\par
\par
```bash\par
whoami\par
```\par
\par
This displays the username associated with the current session.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 9.2 User Information\par
\par
```bash\par
id\par
```\par
\par
The command displays the user's UID, primary group, and supplementary groups.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 9.3 Logged-In Users\par
\par
```bash\par
who\par
```\par
\par
Displays information about users currently logged into the system.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 9.4 Creating a User\par
\par
On systems using standard Linux user-management utilities:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo useradd -m student\par
```\par
\par
A password can be assigned using:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo passwd student\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 9.5 Creating a Group\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo groupadd developers\par
```\par
\par
A user can be added to a supplementary group using:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo usermod -aG developers student\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 10. LINUX FILE PERMISSIONS\par
\par
Linux uses permissions to control access to files and directories.\par
\par
The basic permissions are:\par
\par
* `r` \f1\emdash  Read\par
* `w` \emdash  Write\par
* `x` \emdash  Execute\par
\par
Permissions apply to:\par
\par
* Owner\par
* Group\par
* Others\par
\par
For example:\par
\par
```text\par
-rwxr-xr--\par
```\par
\par
This means:\par
\par
```text\par
Owner  : rwx\par
Group  : r-x\par
Others : r--\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 10.1 chmod\par
\par
The `chmod` command changes permissions.\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
chmod 755 script.sh\par
```\par
\par
Permission values are commonly represented as:\par
\par
```text\par
Read    = 4\par
Write   = 2\par
Execute = 1\par
```\par
\par
Therefore:\par
\par
```text\par
7 = Read + Write + Execute\par
5 = Read + Execute\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
## 10.2 chown\par
\par
The `chown` command changes ownership.\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo chown student file.txt\par
```\par
\par
Permission management is an important part of Linux security.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 11. PROCESS MANAGEMENT\par
\par
A process is a running instance of a program.\par
\par
Linux provides several commands for monitoring and managing processes.\par
\par
## `ps`\par
\par
```bash\par
ps\par
```\par
\par
More detailed information can be displayed using:\par
\par
```bash\par
ps aux\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
## `top`\par
\par
```bash\par
top\par
```\par
\par
Displays real-time process and resource information.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## `kill`\par
\par
A process can be requested to terminate by specifying its process ID:\par
\par
```bash\par
kill PID\par
```\par
\par
Administrators should terminate processes carefully because forcibly ending applications can result in lost or incomplete work.\par
\par
---\par
\par
## `jobs`\par
\par
Displays jobs associated with the current shell:\par
\par
```bash\par
jobs\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 12. SYSTEM MONITORING\par
\par
Linux provides many utilities for examining system resources.\par
\par
## Kernel Information\par
\par
```bash\par
uname -a\par
```\par
\par
## Memory\par
\par
```bash\par
free -h\par
```\par
\par
## Disk Space\par
\par
```bash\par
df -h\par
```\par
\par
## Directory Usage\par
\par
```bash\par
du -sh /home\par
```\par
\par
## System Uptime\par
\par
```bash\par
uptime\par
```\par
\par
These commands provide administrators with information useful for troubleshooting and resource monitoring.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 13. STORAGE MANAGEMENT\par
\par
Storage management involves understanding available disk space, partitions, file systems, and storage usage.\par
\par
Useful commands include:\par
\par
```bash\par
df -h\par
```\par
\par
for file-system capacity, and:\par
\par
```bash\par
du -sh *\par
```\par
\par
for directory/file usage.\par
\par
Another useful command is:\par
\par
```bash\par
lsblk\par
```\par
\par
which displays block devices and their relationships.\par
\par
Storage monitoring is important because insufficient disk space can cause applications and system services to malfunction.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 14. PACKAGE MANAGEMENT\par
\par
Linux distributions provide package-management systems for installing and maintaining software.\par
\par
On Debian- and Ubuntu-based systems, APT is commonly used.\par
\par
Update package information:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo apt update\par
```\par
\par
Upgrade packages:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo apt upgrade\par
```\par
\par
Install software:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo apt install package-name\par
```\par
\par
Remove software:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo apt remove package-name\par
```\par
\par
For example:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo apt install git\par
```\par
\par
Package-management commands differ between distributions.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 15. SERVICE MANAGEMENT\par
\par
Many Linux systems use `systemd` to manage system services.\par
\par
Check a service:\par
\par
```bash\par
systemctl status service-name\par
```\par
\par
Start a service:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo systemctl start service-name\par
```\par
\par
Stop a service:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo systemctl stop service-name\par
```\par
\par
Restart a service:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo systemctl restart service-name\par
```\par
\par
Enable a service during system startup:\par
\par
```bash\par
sudo systemctl enable service-name\par
```\par
\par
Service management is important when operating servers and other long-running systems.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 16. LINUX NETWORKING\par
\par
Linux provides a comprehensive collection of networking tools.\par
\par
## Network Interfaces\par
\par
```bash\par
ip addr\par
```\par
\par
This displays network interfaces and addresses.\par
\par
## Routing\par
\par
```bash\par
ip route\par
```\par
\par
Displays the system's routing table.\par
\par
## Connectivity Testing\par
\par
```bash\par
ping -c 4 example.com\par
```\par
\par
This sends a limited number of ICMP echo requests to test connectivity.\par
\par
## Network Sockets\par
\par
```bash\par
ss\par
```\par
\par
can display socket information and active network connections.\par
\par
Networking commands are useful for configuration, diagnostics, and troubleshooting.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 17. LINUX COMMAND LINE\par
\par
The Linux command line is an interface where commands are entered and executed.\par
\par
A typical command follows this structure:\par
\par
```text\par
command [options] [arguments]\par
```\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
ls -l /home\par
```\par
\par
Here:\par
\par
* `ls` is the command.\par
* `-l` is an option.\par
* `/home` is the argument.\par
\par
Command-line knowledge is one of the most important Linux skills because many administrative operations can be performed without a graphical interface.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 18. ESSENTIAL LINUX COMMANDS\par
\par
## `pwd`\par
\par
Displays the current directory.\par
\par
```bash\par
pwd\par
```\par
\par
## `ls`\par
\par
Lists directory contents.\par
\par
```bash\par
ls\par
```\par
\par
Detailed listing:\par
\par
```bash\par
ls -l\par
```\par
\par
Including hidden files:\par
\par
```bash\par
ls -la\par
```\par
\par
## `cd`\par
\par
Changes directories.\par
\par
```bash\par
cd /home\par
```\par
\par
Return to the parent:\par
\par
```bash\par
cd ..\par
```\par
\par
Return to home:\par
\par
```bash\par
cd ~\par
```\par
\par
## `mkdir`\par
\par
Creates a directory:\par
\par
```bash\par
mkdir LinuxPractice\par
```\par
\par
## `touch`\par
\par
Creates an empty file:\par
\par
```bash\par
touch notes.txt\par
```\par
\par
## `cp`\par
\par
Copies files:\par
\par
```bash\par
cp notes.txt backup.txt\par
```\par
\par
## `mv`\par
\par
Moves or renames files:\par
\par
```bash\par
mv notes.txt linux_notes.txt\par
```\par
\par
## `rm`\par
\par
Removes a file:\par
\par
```bash\par
rm linux_notes.txt\par
```\par
\par
## `cat`\par
\par
Displays file contents:\par
\par
```bash\par
cat notes.txt\par
```\par
\par
## `less`\par
\par
Allows convenient viewing of longer files:\par
\par
```bash\par
less notes.txt\par
```\par
\par
## `grep`\par
\par
Searches text:\par
\par
```bash\par
grep "Linux" notes.txt\par
```\par
\par
## `find`\par
\par
Searches for files and directories:\par
\par
```bash\par
find . -name "*.txt"\par
```\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 19. PIPES AND REDIRECTION\par
\par
One of the most powerful features of the Linux command line is the ability to combine commands.\par
\par
## Output Redirection\par
\par
```bash\par
ls > files.txt\par
```\par
\par
The output of `ls` is written to `files.txt`.\par
\par
## Append\par
\par
```bash\par
echo "Linux Course" >> notes.txt\par
```\par
\par
The output is appended to the file.\par
\par
## Pipe\par
\par
A pipe sends the output of one command to another command:\par
\par
```bash\par
ls | grep ".txt"\par
```\par
\par
Another example:\par
\par
```bash\par
ps aux | grep bash\par
```\par
\par
Pipes allow simple commands to be combined into more advanced workflows.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 20. SHELL AND BASH\par
\par
A shell is a program that interprets commands entered by the user.\par
\par
Common shells include:\par
\par
* Bash\par
* Zsh\par
* Fish\par
* Dash\par
* Ksh\par
\par
Bash stands for **Bourne Again SHell** and is one of the most widely used shells in Linux environments.\par
\par
Bash supports:\par
\par
* Command execution\par
* Variables\par
* Conditions\par
* Loops\par
* Functions\par
* Pipes\par
* Redirection\par
* Command substitution\par
* Job control\par
* Scripting\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 21. BASH VARIABLES\par
\par
Variables store values that can be used by scripts.\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
name="Neil"\par
echo "$name"\par
```\par
\par
Output:\par
\par
```text\par
Neil\par
```\par
\par
Another example:\par
\par
```bash\par
course="Linux Operating System"\par
echo "Course: $course"\par
```\par
\par
Variables make scripts flexible because the same script can work with different values.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 22. USER INPUT AND OUTPUT\par
\par
Bash provides the `read` command for receiving user input.\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
#!/bin/bash\par
\par
echo "Enter your name:"\par
read name\par
\par
echo "Welcome, $name"\par
```\par
\par
The `echo` command is commonly used to display information.\par
\par
This combination of `read` and `echo` allows interactive shell scripts to communicate with users.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 23. CONDITIONAL STATEMENTS\par
\par
Conditional statements allow a script to make decisions.\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
#!/bin/bash\par
\par
number=10\par
\par
if [ "$number" -gt 5 ]\par
then\par
    echo "Number is greater than 5"\par
else\par
    echo "Number is 5 or less"\par
fi\par
```\par
\par
Conditions can be used for:\par
\par
* Checking whether files exist\par
* Comparing numbers\par
* Comparing strings\par
* Validating user input\par
* Making decisions during automation\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 24. LOOPS\par
\par
Loops allow a command or group of commands to be executed repeatedly.\par
\par
## For Loop\par
\par
```bash\par
for i in 1 2 3 4 5\par
do\par
    echo "Number: $i"\par
done\par
```\par
\par
## While Loop\par
\par
```bash\par
count=1\par
\par
while [ "$count" -le 5 ]\par
do\par
    echo "Count: $count"\par
    count=$((count + 1))\par
done\par
```\par
\par
Loops are useful for processing multiple files, performing repetitive operations, and automating administrative tasks.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 25. FUNCTIONS\par
\par
Functions allow groups of commands to be organized into reusable units.\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
greet()\par
\{\par
    echo "Welcome to Linux"\par
\}\par
\par
greet\par
```\par
\par
Functions improve the organization and maintainability of larger scripts.\par
\par
---\par
\par
# 26. BASH SCRIPTING\par
\par
Bash scripting combines Linux commands and programming constructs into executable scripts.\par
\par
A Bash script normally begins with a shebang:\par
\par
```bash\par
#!/bin/bash\par
```\par
\par
A simple script can be created using a text editor:\par
\par
```bash\par
nano system_info.sh\par
```\par
\par
Example:\par
\par
```bash\par
#!/bin/bash\par
\par
echo "Linux System Information"\par
echo "------------------------"\par
\par
echo "Current User:"\par
whoami\par
\par
echo "Current Directory:"\par
pwd\par
\par
echo "Kernel:"\par
uname -r\par
\par
echo "Disk Usage:"\par
df -h\par
\par
echo "Memory:"\par
free -h\par
\par
echo "Date:"\par
date\par
```\par
\par
The script can be made executable:\par
\par
```bash\par
chmod +x system_info.sh\par
```\par
\par
and executed:\par
\par
```bash\par
./system_info.sh\par
```\par
\par
