# 35. LINUX SECURITY FUNDAMENTALS\par
\par
Security is an important part of Linux administration.\par
\par
## Authentication\par
\par
Authentication verifies the identity of a user.\par
\par
## Authorization\par
\par
Authorization determines which resources an authenticated user can access.\par
\par
## File Permissions\par
\par
Permissions restrict access to files and directories.\par
\par
## Least Privilege\par
\par
Users and services should receive only the privileges required to perform their legitimate functions.\par
\par
## Software Updates\par
\par
Keeping software updated is an important maintenance and security practice.\par
\par
## Logging\par
\par
System logs provide information that can assist with troubleshooting and security monitoring.\par
\par
## Firewall\par
\par
Linux systems can use firewall technologies to regulate network traffic.\par
\par
## Secure Administration\par
\par
Administrative privileges should be used carefully. Commands involving system configuration, users, permissions, or deletion should be executed only when necessary and with appropriate authorization.\par
\par
